Subject: free will - gravely serious point

James A. Donald writes:

>In <199310011222.AA09528@mail.swip.net>, felix@hu.se (Felix Ungman)  wrote:
>
>> Yes, I agree that the problem with the notation of Free
>> Will(tm) is the definition of it.
>
>No problem.  Sometimes we speak of freedom from some
>particular undesirable thing - "Your teeth are free from
>cavities", "Freedom from hunger", "free from disease"  

>
>But whenever we do not specify the particular undesirable
>thing we always and invariably mean freedom from coercion. 

>For example when we say a weight lifter is free to lift
>whichever weight he chooses we mean he owns the weights, or
>has permission to use them.  We do not mean that he is
>strong.

Hmmm, I think this is too glib a dismissal of the difficulty
inherent in finding a suitable definition for "free" or "free will".
We can mean a great many things when we say "free", and we don't
need to use function words to do so. (A function word is one such
as the "from" you use above that (as a preposition, auxiliary verb,
or conjunction) expresses a primarily grammatical relationship.)

Here are a some examples of how the word "free" can have different
meanings without the use of additional function words. None of these
are quite the same as using "free" to means the absence of coercion
in choice or action.

I'll be free this evening. <not taken up with commitments or obligations>

He waved with his free hand. <not being used or occupied>

Grab the free end of the rope. <not fastened>

The Congress is know for it's free habits of spending. <not parsimonious>

John Smith was a free safety for that down. <not confined to a particular  
position or place>

Watch the boom, it's free! <capable of moving or turning in any direction>

I saw amazing free tumbling at the Circe de Soliel. <performed without  
apparatus>

He was quite a free speaker. <OUTSPOKEN, FRANK, OPEN>

Many felt her affections were too free. <overly familiar or forward in action  
or attitude, LICENTIOUS>

Those samples are free. <not costing or charging anything>

The open-air ampitheatre is ringed by free columns. <not united with, attached  
to, combined with, or mixed with something else, FREESTANDING>

In this experiment, we wish to show the presense of free oxygen. <uncombined>

This device allows us to measure the number of free electrons in a metal. <not  
permanently attached but able to move about>

The word hats is a free form. <capable of being used alone as a meaningful  
linguistic form>

This is a fairly free translation of Moliere. <not literal or exact>

My favorite event is the free skating. <not restricted by or conforming to  
conventional forms>

With this free wind we'll make good time. <FAVORABLE - used of a wind blowing  
from a direction more than six points from dead ahead>


 - Jeff

