Subject: Operationalism (was Meaninglessness)

James Donald's problems with operationalism stem from his hangups with
the word operation.  Unfortunately, due to his habit of stringing one
non-sequitur after another, I can't tell where he goes off the rails
between:

> The scientific position is that concepts are meaningful
> if they have consequences for the world that are in
> principle observable.
and 
> According to the dictionary [operationalism means]
> "Scientific concepts must be defined in terms of 
> [repeatable] operations" 

These are compatible statements.  Well almost.  The dictionary said
_definable_, not _defined_, which might explain James' problems with
possible, but not yet available, dog detectors.  A dog detector is
definable in principle, although not yet defined in practice, and 
hence an aid in clarifying concepts.

His other problems (like over Noether's theorem) seem to lie with the
relationship between maths and quantum field theory.  He claims that
wavefunctions, state vectors, bras and kets, quantum fields etc are
unobservable in some way that, say, tables and chairs are not.  But the
relative magnitude and phase angles of all the components of a state
vector are observable if the measurement operation is _repeated_ on
identically prepared systems.  Quantum entities like wavefunctions are
as real as the more classical objects.  Since James agrees with a
well-known crackpot (on the intersection of QM and AI: Penrose) there's
not much point continuing on the subject of quantum reality.

