At  9:43 PM 9/30/93 -0700, John K Clark wrote:
>I don't see a
>sharp distinction between animate and inanimate objects.

The question is not how sharp the distinction is, but how useful. A
distinction may have useful thing to say about extremes, while remaining
silent on the borderline cases. (Similarly for conscious/not conscious.)
But I have some sympathy for what you're saying. Bright lines are drawn by
the mind, not the phenomena. But this same move can be used to internalize
the causes Clark (I think?) wants to view as external.
>
>Perhaps your distinction between "cause" and "motivation does
>have merit in everyday situations. "Cause" being reserved for 
>simple systems and "motivation" for more complex systems ,like me. 
>Philosophically however the two words mean the same.

This begs the question.

I do the same thing frequently (perhaps in the first sentence of this
post?), but when I catch myself I realize that I'm arguing about
interpretations of the world rather than the world. Interpretations are
worth arguing about, but present difficulties. What is it about Clark's
that should make me wish to adopt it? (Actually, my distinction between
interpretations and the world is bogus, but I hope my meaning is clear. 'My
map is better than your map!' versus 'There's no river in this territory!'
'Yes there is!')

What practical difference would it make in our lives if we decided we
do/don't have free will? (Where's Mike Price when I need him?) Does
deciding I don't have free will make decisions somehow
harder/easier/unnecessary?

The only hard evidence I've seen shows that people who believe they have
control over their lives are more happy and successful. This says nothing
about the accuracy of their belief (and may have little to do with the
distinction Clark is trying to make), but until some better evidence comes
along, sounds good to me.

