> Eli, could you tell us more about your roomate getting
> a Pennicillin subscription for a viral cold infection?

I'm not sure how list-relevant this is, or whether he wants me going
into detail on his medical history, but a short summary:  He arrived
at the start of the year with a pretty nasty cold.  Though I can't
be certain, it seemed based on his and my symptoms (yeah, I got it
too) that it was a fairly typical viral infection.  But from his
description of his "health care", it wouldn't have mattered if he'd
had pneumonic plague -- it was just, "You're sick?  Here, have some
of this."  It sounded like he came in pretty low in their triage
system.

My understanding is that prescribing a relatively benign drug such
as penicillin is fairly common in situations like this, because
people are unhappy with, "We spent fifty bucks on a throat culture,
and found that we can't do much.  Treat it symptomatically and it
will go away."  And I guess it worked; he said he thought it had
helped.  There are other considerations -- on the upside, this
avoids the time and expense of tests, and may cure asymptomatic
diseases just by chance.  On the other hand, it promotes
antibiotic-resistant strains, and has the usual side-effect and
allergy risks.  If antibiotics were free, safe, and totally
effective, this probably would be a fine idea.

There is also a counterpart to the placebo effect that is worth
mentioning.  Physicians really want to have an effective treatment
for everything, and tend to view positively any attempted treatment
for a given disorder.  I was recently reading a history of the
treatment of various poisonings, and was struck by the consistent
history of fad treatments: long strings of short-lived `antidotes',
the use of dangerous treatments such as convulsant drugs in the face
of evidence that they didn't work.  (Present theory is that unless
you have a specific antidote, you simply minimize the toxin present
and keep the patient alive and their organ systems functioning.)

   Eli   ebrandt@jarthur.claremont.edu

