For everyone's information ...

I am (by part-time profession) a fictionalizer, so when I wrote about having seen
my dossier I was weaving truth with fiction. I _did_ dine out with Tim last
night, but instead of showing me my dossier he simply let me in on the scam 
(hence my post this morning, which was entirely my own idea). I was unsubscribed
to the list from about December last year until last week, so I missed his
initial Blacknet posting. However ...

I was not joking when I said that I have been self-censoring personal items from
net postings and mail list postings for some time. I haven't yet censored them
from private mail to people I know and trust, although I am seriously thinking
that it's about time I began raising their consciousness about PGP.

A few months ago, rather late in the game, I played a thought-experiment that
scared the living shit out of me. I attempted to calculate the bandwidth in terms
of DAT tapes that would be needed to record every telephone conversation in the
UK, my home country. The UK is worth considering because it has approximately
one fifth the population of the US and a comparable level of telecommunications
infrastructure, although virtually all calls are piped via British Telecom's
exchanges (except for the City of Hull PTT and a couple of other weird exceptions).

My guesstimate was between one and ten DAT tapes per System X or System Y exchange
per week would be needed to record every call in the country, and a couple of
Crays would easily be up to the task of performing realtime traffic analysis
on the PSTN, working out the transitive dependencies on communications between 
individuals flagged as "suspicious".

Last night, at Tim's prompting, I tried to work out how easily they could shut 
down Blacknet, should it evolve. The answer was, "very easily". It's trivial to
construct a filter algorithm that can distinguish data transmissions from voice
on the basis of entropy. Check data transmissions off-line to identify fax
headers, and eliminate faxes; you're left with modems. Check phone numbers for
"legitimate" corporate accounts and you're left with a relatively small number
of comms users. (The use of modems in the UK, as in the rest of Europe, is
far behind that in the US: I've seen it said that there are more BBS's in New
York alone than in the whole of Europe.)

A Blacknet clampdown in Europe is therefore practical for the authorities within
the near future. A lot of collateral casualties would be hit, and the fallout would
make the Steve Jackson Games scandal look like a storm in a teacup, but *** it could
be done ***. Right now. Not in five years' time, in all probability, unless 
restrictions are placed on the spread of modems ... but for the short to medium
term, the government can keep a lid on Blacknet by the skin of its teeth.

Something that hasn't come up in this discussion so far is the grading of 
information sources that are amenable to filtration and traffic analysis. I've given
a bit of thought to how it might be possible to filter information to deduce
conclusions from apparently unrelated data. Very often, the grade of information
you can obtain from a channel is inversely proportional to the bandwidth of a 
channel. As an example, imagine a CIA controller monitoring pre-Glastnost Russia.
High grade information is returned by spies or defectors -- in small quantities.
Medium grade information can be obtained by elint -- tracking enemy communications,
cracking cyphers, and so on. But there's a lot more bandwidth to cover, and a 
lot of stations to listen to. Low grade information can be obtained by sifting
through the huge mounds of propaganda emanating from the State broadcasting
apparatus and doing things like deconstructing the stock phrases used to assemble
propaganda speeches; but there's a HUGE information stream and little high-grade
information present in it.

I would view text-scans of the internet as a medium-grade infosource. High grade
information is your personal mailfolder and your private files. Low grade information
is stuff that other people say (about matters other than you) in those newsgroups you 
read ... your participation in them is indicative of temperament, if nothing else.

Anyone else given any thought to this kind of inferential analysis, and ways and
means of grading intelligence from the net? I'm sure it's not a field in its
infancy any more, but the people who know about it probably all work for TLA's ...




